    summary: `Lots of molecules come in two versions that are mirror images of each other — same atoms, same connections, but arranged as "left" and "right" versions, like gloves. This is called <b>chirality</b>, and it matters enormously: one mirror form of a drug can cure while its twin does nothing (or worse — thalidomide's left form treats morning sickness, its right form caused birth defects). Making just one hand is hard. <b>Henri Kagan</b> discovered <i>non-linear effects</i>: with a partly-pure chiral catalyst you can get product far purer than the catalyst itself. <b>Kenso Soai</b> went further and found a reaction whose own product catalyses more of itself — <i>asymmetric autocatalysis</i> — so a starting mixture just 2% leaning left can end up &gt;99% left-handed. It even hints at how life ended up using only left-handed amino acids.`,
