    summary: `In the quantum world, particles can do impossible things: pass straight through walls (<b>tunnelling</b>) and only sit on specific energy steps like rungs of a ladder (<b>quantisation</b>). For decades, this was thought to apply only to microscopic things. In the mid-1980s, Clarke, Devoret and Martinis built <b>superconducting circuits containing Josephson junctions</b> — ultra-thin insulating gaps in a superconductor — cooled near absolute zero. They showed the whole circuit acts like a single artificial atom: its collective state tunnels through energy barriers it classically couldn't cross, and absorbs microwaves only in exact energy chunks. That discovery is the direct ancestor of today's <b>superconducting qubits</b>, the heart of Google and IBM's quantum computers.`,
