    summary: `Claudia Goldin spent decades assembling data others had ignored — census records, business archives, prescription histories — to tell the long story of women in the American economy. Her findings dismantled lazier explanations. Women's participation isn't a simple rise: it <b>dips then climbs in a U-shape</b> as economies grow (women worked before industrialisation, got pushed out, then returned with services and education). The 1970s "<b>quiet revolution</b>" came when the contraceptive pill let women plan long careers. And the stubborn modern pay gap is driven less by discrimination in identical jobs and more by <b>"greedy work"</b> — jobs that pay giant premiums for being always available — colliding with parenthood: the "<b>child penalty</b>" hits mothers' earnings for a decade and accounts for most of today's gap.`,
